# What I learned from this problem

* Always remove fractions by cross multiplication.
* Convert subarray conditions into prefix expressions.
* If both sides contain the same repeated expression, define a new prefix value (`F(i)` here) to simplify the inequality.
* When a problem repeatedly asks **"How many previous prefix values satisfy a comparison?"**, think of data structures like **Fenwick Tree**, **Segment Tree**, or an **ordered set/map**.
* If a data structure requires indices but your values are large or negative, use **coordinate compression**.
* Look for additional constraints (like `odd > 0`) that can sometimes be handled separately by organizing or grouping prefixes instead of checking them repeatedly.

---

One final piece of advice: **don't memorize this code.** Memorize the chain of ideas:

> **Fraction → Cross multiply → Prefix sums → Transform into one prefix value → Prefix comparison → Coordinate compression → Fenwick Tree.**

If you remember that sequence, you'll be able to rediscover the solution months later, even if you've forgotten every line of the implementation.
